{
  "dom_id": "step-q276365",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\u03a6(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Y\u223cN(-1,{2}^{2}),$ compute $P(Y\\ge 1).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8849\\,$ | $\\,0.8869\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ |\n\n$P(Y\\ge 1)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Given a normal random variable $Y\u223cN(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge y)=P(Z\\ge \\frac{y-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z\u223cN(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =-1$ and $\\sigma =2.$\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 1) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{1-(-1)}{2}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/4cdc40bd57d5f855afa08ee21b3bb9eeeefca8c09bd4d857cf3f3b2514693110.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $\u03a6(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 1) & =1-P(Z<1) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1) \\\\  & =1-\u03a6(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\n\u03a6(1)=0.8413.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 1) & =1-\u03a6(1) \\\\  & =1-0.8413 \\\\  & =0.1587.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 1)=0.1587.\n$$",
